\description{
Reads and normalises a New Zealand Treasury tax outturn release. The parser
maps the official \code{AllRevenue} and \code{AllReceipts} workbook sheets to a single
long-form table while retaining the distinction between accrual revenue and
cash receipts. It also keeps the source's unconsolidated rows, Section III
consolidation eliminations, core Crown total, and whole-of-government total
as separate, explicitly labelled observations. This makes it possible to
compare tax measures without silently mixing accounting or consolidation
regimes.
}
\details{
The default is the current byte-pinned tax outturn release in the package
catalogue. An existing XLSX workbook or canonical CSV may be supplied for a
deterministic offline replay; supplying \code{file} never performs network I/O.
Without a local file, retrieval is HTTPS-only from the official Treasury
host and requires \code{accept_terms = TRUE} after reviewing
\code{\link[=nz_fiscal_terms]{nz_fiscal_terms()}}. Downloaded bytes are checked against the catalogue
SHA-256 before they are parsed.

For XLSX input, \code{measure = "both"} parses \code{AllRevenue} first and
\code{AllReceipts} second. \code{measure = "revenue"} or \code{"receipts"} parses only the
corresponding sheet. Rows retain workbook row and monthly-column order;
the function does not sort, aggregate, interpolate, or otherwise adjust
values. For canonical CSV input, the file is read as an already normalised
result with the schema documented under \code{Value}; selecting one measure
filters rows without changing their input order.

\code{date} is the monthly observation date represented by the source header.
\code{fiscal_year} is derived from that date using the Treasury July--June
convention: July through December belong to the fiscal year ending the next
calendar year, and January through June belong to the fiscal year ending in
the current calendar year. The resulting label is \code{"YYYY/YY"}. Revenue is
labelled \code{accounting_basis = "accrual"} and receipts
\code{accounting_basis = "cash"}; all values use \code{unit = "NZD million"}.

Revenue rows dated before 1 June 2007 have
\code{writeoff_regime = "net_of_tax_writeoffs"}; revenue rows dated on or after
1 June 2007 have \code{writeoff_regime = "gross_of_tax_writeoffs"}. Receipts
always have \code{writeoff_regime = "not_applicable_to_cash_receipts"}. The
break is represented as source metadata and does not rewrite historical
values.

Consolidation scope is assigned from the source section and series label.
Rows outside \verb{Section III:} are \code{"unconsolidated"}. Within Section III,
\code{"CORE CROWN TAX"} is \code{"core Crown consolidated"},
\code{"FULLY CONSOLIDATED TAX"} is \code{"whole-of-government consolidated"}, and
the recognised elimination labels are assigned
\code{"consolidation elimination"}: \code{"GST ON DEPARTMENTAL EXPENSES"},
\code{"OTHER CROWN GST"}, \code{"INCOME TAX FROM NZSF AND GSF"},
\code{"CORE CROWN EMPLOYER SUPERANNUATION CONTRIBUTION TAX"},
\code{"CORE CROWN APPROVED ISSUER LEVY"}, \code{"GAMING DUTY FROM NZLC"},
\code{"INCOME TAX FROM SOES AND CES"}, and
\code{"OTHER CROWN EMPLOYER SUPERANNUATION CONTRIBUTION TAX"}. An unknown
Section III label is an error rather than an inferred scope, so source
schema drift is visible to callers.

Parsed results use an in-memory cache keyed by the raw SHA-256, release,
revision policy, parser and schema versions, and parser options. Raw
snapshots are hash-addressed below \code{cache_dir} and are subject to the
package's raw-cache bounds. The default cache is
\code{tools::R_user_dir("readfiscalnz", "cache")/v1}; using \code{tempfile()} for
\code{cache_dir} is useful for isolated examples and tests. Reading a local file
can create package-managed files below \code{cache_dir}, but does not modify the
input file. A live request is refused when \code{accept_terms = FALSE}; when it
is enabled, transport is timeout- and size-bounded, permits only HTTPS
redirects, validates that the final effective URL remains on the Treasury
host, and uses bounded attempts for transport errors and HTTP 408, 429, 500,
502, 503, and 504 responses.

Invalid argument values, an absent or unsupported release, a missing
workbook sheet, an empty or malformed workbook, a non-CSV/XLSX artifact,
an artifact outside \code{max_bytes}, an invalid file signature, an unexpected
